\subsection{Estimation bias: loss visibility versus spatial resolution}
%% <<GAP:DECOMPOSITION>> DRAFT with final defensible-U numbers (decomposition_
%% defensibleU.csv + synth_factorial_decomposition.csv); polish prose, add CO2 row.
On Memmingen the copperplate-to-baseline cost gap of \result{total_pct}\,\%
(\CP\ $\to$ \Lone) decomposes to machine precision into a loss main effect of
\result{loss_pct}\,\% of the gap, a topology main effect of \result{topo_pct}\,\%,
and interaction of \result{interaction_pct}\,\% (defensible trunk $U$; the single
indefensible terminal-pipe multiplier of v1 removed). Loss visibility carries the
result: a copperplate reproduces the cost level once handed the aggregate loss it
cannot itself compute (\CPL), whereas resolving spatial topology without loss
(\NDz) recovers almost none of the gap. Spatial resolution matters as the
\emph{mechanism} that makes loss endogenous, not through routing. Across the 42
synthetic networks the same holds emphatically: loss explains a median
\result{synth_loss_median}\,\% of the gap and topology a median
\result{synth_topo_median}\,\% (never above 0.6\,\%, no network above 10\,\%),
over node counts, pipe lengths, demand heterogeneity and storage. The multiplier
correction leaves the split unchanged (v1 inflated-$U$: 96/4; defensible-$U$:
95.5/3.9), so the conclusion is not an artefact of the loss calibration R2.4 queried.
\input{tables/tab_decomposition}
%% Figure F6(a,b)

\subsection{Decision regret and physical feasibility}
%% <<GAP:REGRET>> DRAFT (bias_regret.csv + regret_sensitivity.csv); finalise numbers
%% on defensible-U, add decision-divergence metrics (HP hours, producer shares).
Estimation bias and decision regret diverge sharply, and for the copperplate they
carry \emph{opposite signs}. Its schedule appears \result{cp_bias_pct}\,\% cheaper
than the baseline (bias), yet re-costing that loss-ignoring schedule under the common
forward physics makes it \result{cp_regret_pct}\,\% more expensive to execute
(regret): a copperplate is a biased estimator \emph{and} an incompetent controller,
because it under-generates for the network losses it never saw. Levels that model
losses (\Ltwo--\Lsix) have regret $\approx$ bias -- their schedules are deliverable.
The sign of the copperplate regret is invariant across the marginal-heat-cost range
(47--90\,EUR/MWh) and the demand-disaggregation rule (\(<\)0.3\,pp), so it is not an
artefact of either assumption. This is the decision-relevant reading of fidelity that
model-to-model bias alone cannot give (R1.2): use the simplest model for scheduling
only if it is loss-aware; never a copperplate.
\input{tables/tab_regret}
%% Figure F6(c)

